WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.060
When we think of the largest retailers in
the world, the first companies that come to

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:05.880
mind are Walmart and Amazon.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:12.540
If you thought a bit harder, you’d probably
name Target, Costco, Sams Club, and Home Depot.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:13.809
But what about IKEA?

00:00:13.809 --> 00:00:20.210
We all know about IKEA and have probably shopped
there in the past, but for most people, IKEA

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:22.860
is not the first retailer that comes to mind.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:27.390
And this isn’t a coincidence either, for
how large IKEA is, they keep an extremely

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:28.760
low profile.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:33.170
You don’t see many articles talking about
how IKEA is a monopoly or that its founder

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:35.070
is actually Lex Luthor.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:38.989
We also don’t see analysts giving outlooks
for IKEA’s revenue or putting out price

00:00:38.989 --> 00:00:42.440
predictions for IKEA stock because the company
is privately owned.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:47.180
IKEA is one of those things that we all know
about but we never talk about.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:50.750
And that’s a shame given how impressive
and substantial their business is.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:59.659
So, here’s the story of Ingvar Kamprad and
how he became the $58.7 billion shadow retailer.

00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:05.230
Taking a look back, Ingvar was born quite
a while ago on March 30, 1926, in Pjätteryd

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:06.230
Sweden.

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:10.110
He grew up on a small farm called Elmtaryd
located in the village of Agunnaryd.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:15.210
Ingvar was born right before the great depression
and his parents weren’t particularly wealthy,

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:18.470
so the depression was a pretty rough experience
for the family.

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:21.850
Everyone in their little village was just
trying to survive on a day-to-day basis by

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:24.530
saving every penny and wasting nothing.

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:28.100
Most kids were expected to work on the farm
during the day and help out with house chores

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:29.310
in the evening.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:33.380
But despite the Kamprad family’s desperate
state, they never forced Ingvar to help out.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:37.330
It appears that though they couldn’t give
Ingvar financial freedom, they wanted him

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:40.110
to have the freedom of spending his time how
he wanted.

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:44.670
Ingvar was never too interested in working
on the farm or doing house chores, but from

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:47.850
a very early age, he wanted to help his family
financially.

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:52.380
So, at 5, Ingvar decided to start selling
matches to his neighbors for pennies.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:57.170
Ingvar barely made any money from this endeavor
given the cheap nature of matches, but it’s

00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:00.890
crazy to think that Ingvar levitated towards
business at age 5.

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:05.450
Fortunately, as Ingvar grew older, he delved
into some more profitable ventures.

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:10.869
Ingvar sold pens, pencils, Christmas tree
decorations, and most importantly fish.

00:02:10.869 --> 00:02:14.640
Selling fish was the first time that Ingvar
addressed a gap in the market.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:19.090
You see, many farmers in his village didn’t
have fishing licenses, but Ingvar’s father

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:20.090
did.

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:23.700
So, catching fish and selling it to these
farmers was a rather profitable idea.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:28.510
Ingvar even got his father to become an investor
in the business and buy him larger nets which

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:30.920
substantially increased his yield.

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:34.301
Despite his side hustle going decently well,
Ingvar’s parents wanted Ingvar to complete

00:02:34.301 --> 00:02:39.980
basic education, so they sent Ingvar to a
nearby boarding school at age 14.

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:43.150
School was quite hard for Ingvar though because
he was dyslexic.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:47.800
However, a promise from his father motivated
Ingvar to work super hard at school.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.930
Ingvar’s father promised that if Ingvar
got good grades in school and graduated, he

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:55.069
would pay the registration fees for setting
up Ingvar’s first business.

00:02:55.069 --> 00:02:58.840
Ingvar put his head down and worked his way
through school by age 17.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:03.190
And as promised, his father paid for Ingvar’s
business registration fees and Ingvar had

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:04.700
the freedom to choose the name.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:07.370
Ingvar chose to keep it simple and personal.

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:11.640
He based the first two letters of the name
on his initials IK, and he based the third

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:14.530
and fourth letters on the village and farm
he was born on.

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:17.350
E for Elmtaryd and A for Agunnaryd.

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:21.620
And with that, Ingvar had launched IKEA at
age 17 in 1943.

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:25.540
Ingvar had saved up a decent amount of money
throughout his childhood, but his savings

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:28.510
were nothing in comparison to established
retailers in the area.

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:32.490
So, Ingvar focused on starting a business
that had super low costs.

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:36.290
His first idea was to create a mail-order
business that sold wallets, photo frames,

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:38.290
pens, and other small items.

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:42.660
This business model allowed Ingvar to completely
eliminate storage costs, but similar to his

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:46.940
match business, Ingvar barely made any money
due to the cheap nature of the items he was

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:47.940
selling.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:51.330
So, in 1948, Ingvar decided to expand to furniture.

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:55.629
Even with furniture, Ingvar stuck to the mail-order
business model to keep costs low.

00:03:55.629 --> 00:03:59.300
He delivered the furniture using trucks and
trains and his business started to grow quite

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:00.300
rapidly.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:03.550
But, his personal life was a lot more questionable.

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:07.640
Around this time, Ingvar joined a group called
the New Sweden Movement led by a man named

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:08.930
Per Engdahl.

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:14.080
Per Engdahl was a fascist and this was during
the height of world war 2, so Ingvar had basically

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:15.989
joined a youth Facist group.

00:04:15.989 --> 00:04:19.740
He later explained that this decision was
largely influenced by his father and grandmother

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:22.120
given that he joined the group at age 16.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:26.350
Ingvar’s primary role within the organization
was to raise funds and recruit new members

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:27.350
for the group.

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:32.620
Ingvar’s involvement didn’t public till
50 years later in 1994 though, when Per Engdahl’s

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:34.350
personal letters were made public.

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:38.940
We don’t know when Ingvar left the organization,
but there’s evidence suggesting that Ingvar

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:42.410
remained friends with Engdahl up until the
early 1950s.

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:47.280
Ingvar did publicly apologize for his involvement
and acknowledged that it was a massive mistake,

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:50.010
but this is nonetheless a dark spot of his
life.

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:54.140
Anyway, going back to IKEA, it didn’t take
long for Ingvar to double down on the furniture

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:57.010
business given the much larger profit per
unit.

00:04:57.010 --> 00:05:01.669
By the early 1950s, Ingvar removed everything
else from IKEA’s catalog, and IKEA officially

00:05:01.669 --> 00:05:03.720
became a furniture company.

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:07.420
About a decade after starting the business,
Ingvar opened up IKEA’s very first showroom

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:09.130
in 1953.

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:13.680
The showroom was especially effective at converting
shoppers into paying customers.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:19.660
But, as IKEA grew, established furniture players
weren’t exactly pleased to see another competitor

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:20.780
entering the scene.

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:24.350
So, they banded together and tried to take
down IKEA.

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:27.590
They called up IKEA’s suppliers and told
them that they would stop buying furniture

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:30.320
from them if they continued to supply IKEA.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:34.610
In fear of preserving their own businesses,
the suppliers bent to this request and stopped

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:35.610
supplying IKEA.

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:40.669
I’m sure this was an extremely stressful
time for Ingvar, but some of the best ideas

00:05:40.669 --> 00:05:45.250
emerge under the most stressful circumstances,
and this couldn’t be truer for Ingvar.

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:48.950
Since he couldn’t buy furniture from the
popular suppliers, Ingvar decided to design

00:05:48.950 --> 00:05:51.720
his own furniture that was simple and cheap.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:55.509
Ingvar made a couple of deals with foreign
suppliers for the materials and with that,

00:05:55.509 --> 00:05:57.550
Ingvar was back in business.

00:05:57.550 --> 00:06:01.430
Ironically, this experience allowed Ingvar
to cut out the middle man suppliers which

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:05.100
only further reduced the price of his furniture
making IKEA even more popular.

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:09.690
Now that IKEA was making their own furniture,
Ingvar had more freedom when it come to how

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:11.460
the furniture was packaged.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:15.419
And that brings us to IKEA’s breakthrough
innovation which is of course their flat boxed

00:06:15.419 --> 00:06:16.440
DIY furniture.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:21.160
By shifting the assembly process to the consumer,
Ingvar was able to drastically reduce shipping

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:22.250
and labor costs.

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:26.570
Now, of course, this completely cut out the
premium furniture buyer market.

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:29.930
Premium furniture buyers weren’t particularly
impressed with IKEA’s choice of materials

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:31.690
or the build it yourself model.

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:35.850
However, for the vast majority of us peasants,
acceptable quality furniture for super low

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:38.020
prices is more than enough.

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:41.759
This new flat boxed design also meant that
IKEA didn’t need to store their merchandise

00:06:41.759 --> 00:06:44.070
at warehouses and ship it to customers.

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:47.100
They could just store it in the showrooms
and have customers take it home with them

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:48.960
which reduced costs even further.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:53.610
In 1965, Ingvar opened IKEA’s first retail
outlet in Stockholm Sweden.

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:58.400
IKEA had already expanded internationally
a few years before this in 1963 with the opening

00:06:58.400 --> 00:06:59.800
of a showroom in Norway.

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:03.720
But, the modern IKEA didn’t make its debut
till 1965.

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:08.210
Around the same time, Ingvar also opened the
first IKEA restaurant in 1960.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:11.901
Ingvar noticed that customers generally left
the store during lunchtime to eat at one of

00:07:11.901 --> 00:07:13.229
the restaurants across the street.

00:07:13.229 --> 00:07:17.270
So, in an effort to bleed fewer customers
during lunchtime, Ingvar opened a kitchen

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:20.120
which turned out to be a massive success.

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:23.430
Just as everything was progressing forward
though, Ingvar would be hit with some bad

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:24.430
luck.

00:07:24.430 --> 00:07:28.410
In 1970, an electrical problem would start
a fire at the Stockholm IKEA.

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:33.550
The fire caused a lot of damage and was quite
expensive to repair, but somehow, even this

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:35.190
turned into a good thing.

00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:38.479
Given that a large portion of the store had
to be shut down while it was being repaired,

00:07:38.479 --> 00:07:41.800
Ingvar introduced a self-service area that
he hoped would reduce congestion and give

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:43.590
customers more freedom.

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:48.070
And as we now know, this became a permanent
change that also reduced costs.

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:51.940
Throughout the rest of the decade, IKEA focused
on opening retail outlets outside of Scandinavia

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:54.190
including Switzerland and Germany.

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:58.830
IKEA opened their first US store in 1985,
and they’ve exploded internationally ever

00:07:58.830 --> 00:07:59.870
since.

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:05.220
By the end of the century, IKEA had grown
to 114 outlets spread across 25 countries.

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:09.990
And that brings us to their last major avenue
of growth which was the internet.

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:13.699
In 2000, IKEA began selling their furniture
over the internet and this quickly cemented

00:08:13.699 --> 00:08:16.780
them as the world’s largest furniture retailer.

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:20.940
Given how enormous IKEA is today, I don’t
think you’d be surprised to hear that Ingvar

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:23.440
was worth $23 billion by March of 2010.

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:29.740
And his wealth grew to a peak of $58.7 billion
in June of 2015 making him the 8th richest

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:30.840
person in the world.

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:35.020
But, Ingvar hated the publicity he got for
his wealth and he actively tried to avoid

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:36.020
it.

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:39.849
Ingvar set up a super convoluted set of holding
companies and foundations that were all independent

00:08:39.849 --> 00:08:40.849
of each other.

00:08:40.849 --> 00:08:49.360
Ingvar 
felt that this was the best way to ensure

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:52.800
that one of his children or a future leader
of IKEA didn’t screw up the business by

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:53.911
making some sort of rash decision.

00:08:53.911 --> 00:08:54.911
Ingvar spread his wealth across these holding
companies and made it so that no major decision

00:08:54.911 --> 00:08:55.911
could be made without approval from all of
them.

00:08:55.911 --> 00:08:58.410
He also made it so that neither he nor his
children could withdraw money from these holding

00:08:58.410 --> 00:08:59.410
companies.

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:04.000
So, on paper Ingvar no longer had access to
this money and his net worth was slashed to

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:05.290
a little over $3 billion.

00:09:05.290 --> 00:09:09.320
This didn’t really matter that much to him
though given that he never lived like a billionaire

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:10.680
in the first place.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:15.270
In fact, not only did he live a modest life,
but he was actually insanely frugal.

00:09:15.270 --> 00:09:19.000
On the surface, he lived in a modest house
and only flew economy and stayed at budget

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:20.190
hotels.

00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:23.430
But his frugal habits stretched much further
than just that.

00:09:23.430 --> 00:09:28.360
Ingvar hated paying for haircuts in the Netherlands
because they often ran him up to $27.

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:32.370
So, whenever Ingvar visited an IKEA store
in a third-world country, he would just get

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:33.430
a haircut there.

00:09:33.430 --> 00:09:35.870
He also purchased a lot of clothes at nearby
flea markets.

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:39.470
I think we can all agree that he’s really
quite a character.

00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:44.470
Unfortunately, Ingvar passed away a few years
ago on January 27, 2018.

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:49.070
But he was 91 years old at the time, and there’s
no doubt that he lived an extremely productive

00:09:49.070 --> 00:09:50.490
and accomplished life.

